cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Predicting an ECG model's positive predictive value from a clinic's prevalence: a retrospective measurement across hospitals and care settings"
message: If you use this repository, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Abbou
    given-names: Ruben
repository-code: https://github.com/rabbou/ecg-conformal-shift
# v1.0.0 and v1.1.0 are tags of earlier articles; this one is cited as 2.0.0, and
# date-released is written when that tag is made at the merge.
version: 2.0.0
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Across {{ppv_gap_cells}} transfers of a threshold between hospitals, care
  settings and corpora, the positive predictive value recomputed by Bayes'
  theorem at the target's true prevalence differed from the observed value by a median
  of {{ppv_gap_median_ci}} percentage points, against {{ppv_control_median_ci}} in
  controls, and fell outside the observed value's 95% confidence interval in
  {{ppv_outside}} of transfers, overestimating it in some and underestimating it in others. At Columbia, the network
  discriminated nearly as well among outpatients as among inpatients (AUROC
  {{auroc_resnet_out}} and {{auroc_resnet_in}}), but outpatients with and without structural
  heart disease both scored lower, so at the inpatient threshold sensitivity
  among outpatients was {{sens_resnet_out}} and the observed positive predictive value
  {{col_ppv_obs_ci}}, where the recomputation gave {{col_ppv_rec}}. After threshold
  recalibration on {{vo_n}} separate outpatients, sensitivity was {{vo_sens_resnet}}. The transfers cover four structural heart
  disease models in EchoNext, an infarction network transferred from PTB-XL to
  hospitals in Chongqing and Shandong, and a rotation of five public corpora
  on five rhythm and conduction diagnoses.
keywords:
  - electrocardiography
  - structural heart disease
  - positive predictive value
  - decision threshold
  - sensitivity
  - spectrum effect
  - distribution shift
